# A group's header never redefines a name the unit's own C takes from its own
# headers (design.md §4.19; CLAUDE.md §7; defect 361, 2026-10-06). A unit
# includes the runtime and `hero_os.h`, then the headers its program's groups
# name, then `<stdint.h>` and `<math.h>` (`runtime/heroes_standard.h`, panel
# 205's R3: read before the groups until 2026-10-10, they latched the C
# library's switches), then every line the compiler wrote, so a macro one
# of those headers defined, or a header it includes, rewrote that C: on this
# Mac, `#define hero_print_int(x) nothing` was *internal error* at exit 2,
# `HERO_RUNTIME_ABI 99` a false *heroes_runtime.h is from another compiler*,
# `main other_main` a link with no `main`, and `INT64_C(c) 0` a program
# printing 0 where it said 42, at exit 0.
#
# **The groups' includes stand between `runtime/heroes_guard_open.h` and
# `runtime/heroes_guard_close.h`**, which save and give back every name in
# `emit/guarded_names.hero`: every word the runtime's two headers write, every
# name C11 gives `<stdbool.h>` and `<stddef.h>`, read before the groups, the
# types and functions of `<stdint.h>` and `<math.h>` and the ten of their
# macros the unit's own C writes, and `main`. A macro of those two the unit
# never writes is left to the headers, since under the guard a group's
# `#define MY_MAX INT32_MAX` would have lost `INT32_MAX` with the region, and
# the ten are defined again after it (`runtime/heroes_standard.h`). A name
# the program's groups write (`bound_names`), a function,
# constant, record, field or releaser of theirs, is pushed again just before
# the close when the list holds it, so the close gives back the header's own
# definition: `LIMIT` bound from a macro keeps the header's value, and so would
# a binding of `sqrt` from a header that makes it a macro. A program that binds
# no header but the compiler's own (`emit/externs.hero`'s `is_own`) has no
# guard at all, and a unit's text grows by its two includes.
# Such a name is saved and undefined around `heroes_standard.h` too
# (`read_after`, defect 2056), and whose macro it is, its own header's or
# another group's, is asked of clang before a unit compiles (`cli/bound_macro`).
#
# **What it does not hold, and why**: a name the unit defines itself, a
# mangled function, field, local or label, which a header's macro would
# rewrite at its definition and every use alike, so the unit fails to compile
# rather than mean something else; and a name C reserves for the
# implementation, which a system header may define on purpose. Measured
# before it was chosen: reading every identifier of the unit instead, the
# route this defect's first draft took, cost the compiler's own build 32.3
# billion instructions more (824.0 to 856.3) and its test unit, 59 MB with
# 37,030 names on one `_Pragma` line, killed Apple clang 21 (`Illegal
# instruction: 4`). The probe units (`emit/layout_*`, `ffi_asked`,
# `cli/header_types`) include the groups alone and ask about the header's own
# names, so they are not guarded.
#
# **The second half of that paragraph was false of the compiler's own C, and
# is corrected here** (defect 2333, 2026-11-08): a name C reserves for the
# implementation that the C this compiler writes holds, a builtin, a keyword
# or a predefined macro, is no header's to define. `#define
# __builtin_add_overflow(a, b, r) ((*(r) = (a) + (b)), 0)` in the header a
# group names turned an `i64` sum past the top into `-9223372036854775808` at
# exit 0, and `#define _Static_assert(c, m)` let a `double` function bound
# `-> i64` build and print `1`; in a probe, the same macros turned a record's
# `ffi_field_type` into an internal error at exit 2. So those names, the list
# `guarded_names.reserved` holds, are saved and given back around the groups
# by the guard's two files, around the C library `heroes_standard.h` reads,
# and around every probe's headers (`guarded_names.reserved_around`); the
# tests below hold every emitter module and the runtime's headers to the
# list. A system header defining one does so only where clang does not
# compile C (`guarded_names.hero`, measured on this Mac's SDK and clang).

use ast
use token
use source
use parse
use emit/guarded_names
use emit/c_words
use emit/externs as emit_externs
use emit/checks_raised
use emit/header_region
use emit/header_state
use emit/deprecation
use cli/process
use cli/strings

# The line before the groups' includes, and the line after them.
constant OPEN_LINE: str
    "#include \"heroes_guard_open.h\""

constant CLOSE_LINE: str
    "#include \"heroes_guard_close.h\""

# The unit's first line, which says it reads the C library's standard headers
# after its groups, and the line that reads them (defect 568).
constant FIRST_LINE: str
    "#include \"heroes_unit.h\""

constant STANDARD_LINE: str
    "#include \"heroes_standard.h\""

# The macros the unit's own C writes from `<stdint.h>` and `<math.h>`, each
# guarded and defined again after the groups by `runtime/heroes_standard.h`.
constant STANDARD_USED: [str]
    [
        "INT64_C"
        "UINT64_C"
        "INT64_MIN"
        "HUGE_VAL"
        "INT8_C"
        "INT8_MIN"
        "INT16_C"
        "INT16_MIN"
        "INT32_C"
        "INT32_MIN"
    ]

# The groups' include lines as the unit writes them: inside the region a
# header's own code is judged in (`emit/header_region.hero`, defect 569),
# the floating-point state saved (`emit/header_state.hero`, defect 582),
# between the two guard includes, each name of the list the program binds
# pushed again before the close, and after it the packing and every other
# state a header can leave set given back; after the region every warning a
# check of a binding rests on raised again, whatever a header's pragma did
# to it (`emit/checks_raised.hero`, defect 570); nothing where there are none.
function around(lines: [str], bound: {str: bool}) -> [str]
    if lines.len() == 0
        return lines
    out: [str] @= strings.with_all(list: header_region.opening(), more: header_state.SAVED)
    out @ out.push(OPEN_LINE)

    for line in lines
        out @ out.push(line)

    for name in guarded_names.names()
        if !bound[name].is_err()
            out @ out.push("#pragma push_macro(\"" + name + "\")")

    out @ out.push(CLOSE_LINE)
    out @ strings.with_all(list: out, more: header_state.GIVEN_BACK)
    out @ out.push(header_region.CLOSING)

    for raised in checks_raised.lines()
        out @ out.push(raised)

    # And a header's deprecations quiet over the rest of the unit, the
    # program's lines as the compiler's (`emit/deprecation.hero`, panel 208).
    out @ out.push(deprecation.QUIET)

    for line in deprecation.BESIDE
        out @ out.push(line)

    return out

# `heroes_standard.h` read with each name of the list the program binds saved
# and undefined, given back after (defect 2056): bound as `sqrt`, `#define sqrt
# my_sqrt` over a `float my_sqrt(float)` rewrote `<math.h>`'s, at exit 2.
function read_after(bound: {str: bool}) -> [str]
    kept: [str] @= []

    for name in guarded_names.names()
        if !bound[name].is_err()
            kept @ kept.push(name)

    out: [str] @= []

    for name in kept
        out @ out.push("#pragma push_macro(\"" + name + "\")")
        out @ out.push("#undef " + name)

    out @ out.push(STANDARD_LINE)

    for name in kept
        out @ out.push("#pragma pop_macro(\"" + name + "\")")

    return out

# Every C name the program's groups take from a header between the guards:
# a function's or constant's own name, a record's, its C tag and its fields,
# and each releaser a mark names, on the result or a parameter. Not a
# parameter's name nor a type, which are the unit's own words: `bool` in a
# signature is the language's, and pushing it again would hand the unit a
# group's `#define bool`. A binding of the compiler's own headers, the
# library's of `hero_os.h` among them, is no group's: its names keep the
# runtime's meaning, whatever a group defines.
function bound_names(s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl]) -> {str: bool}
    out: {str: bool} @= {}

    for decl in decls
        header = header_of(decl)

        if !header.is_err() && !emit_externs.is_own(emit_externs.unquoted(s, span: header.must()))
            spans: [token.Span] @= [decl.name]

            match decl.kind
                .function_decl fd =>
                    for mark in [fd.value.owned_result, fd.value.acquires_result, fd.value.retains_result]
                        if !mark.is_err()
                            spans @ spans.push(mark.must())

                    for param in fd.value.params
                        for mark in [param.owned, param.acquires, param.transfers, param.retains]
                            if !mark.is_err()
                                spans @ spans.push(mark.must())
                .record_decl rec =>
                    if !rec.tag.is_err()
                        spans @ spans.push(rec.tag.must())

                    for field in rec.fields
                        spans @ spans.push(field.name)
                .constant_decl | .variant_decl | .test_decl => _ = 0

            for span in spans
                c_words.words_into(@out, text: source.span_text(s, span))

    return out

# The header a declaration's group named, or none.
function header_of(decl: ast.Decl) -> token.Span?
    match decl.kind
        .function_decl fd          => return fd.value.header
        .constant_decl k           => return k.header
        .record_decl rec           => return rec.header
        .variant_decl | .test_decl => return fail(code: "none", msg: "no header")

    return fail(code: "none", msg: "no header")

## Tests

# The groups' includes alone are guarded, a bound name the list holds is
# pushed again before the close, the state a header can leave set given
# back after it, and none are nothing.
test "the groups' includes stand between the guard's two, a bound name of the list pushed again"
    none: [str] = []
    bound: {str: bool} @= {}
    assert around(none, bound: bound).len() == 0
    bound["INT64_C"] @ true
    bound["LIMIT"] @ true
    opened = strings.with_all(list: header_region.opening(), more: header_state.SAVED)
    guarded = strings.with_all(
        list: opened,
        more: [OPEN_LINE, "#include <a.h>", "#pragma push_macro(\"INT64_C\")", CLOSE_LINE]
    )
    head = strings.with_all(list: guarded, more: header_state.GIVEN_BACK).push(header_region.CLOSING)
    got = around(["#include <a.h>"], bound: bound)
    assert got.len() == head.len() + checks_raised.lines().len() + 1 + deprecation.BESIDE.len()
    assert got.slice(from: 0, to: head.len()) == head
    assert got[head.len()] == "#pragma clang diagnostic error \"-Wreturn-type\""
    assert got[got.len() - 2 - deprecation.BESIDE.len()] == checks_raised.lines()[checks_raised.lines().len() - 1]
    assert got[got.len() - 1 - deprecation.BESIDE.len()] == deprecation.QUIET

# The two runtime files save and give back this list, in its order, and no
# other name: a line added to one and not here is named.
test "the runtime's guard files hold the list, the open saving and the close giving back"
    listed = guarded_names.names()
    assert c_words.pragmas(text: read_file("runtime/heroes_guard_open.h").must(), word: "push_macro") == listed
    assert c_words.pragmas(text: read_file("runtime/heroes_guard_close.h").must(), word: "pop_macro") == listed

# Every word the runtime's headers write is in the list, so a runtime macro's
# expansion is guarded too; a word the runtime gains is named here.
test "the list holds every word the runtime's headers write"
    listed: {str: bool} @= {}

    for name in guarded_names.names()
        listed[name] @ true

    missing: [str] @= []

    for path in ["runtime/heroes_runtime.h", "runtime/hero_os.h"]
        for word in sort(keys(c_words.used(read_file(path).must())))
            if listed[word].is_err()
                missing @ missing.push(word)

    assert missing.join(" ") == ""

# The premise the list's C11 half rests on: the standard headers a unit reads
# before its groups are clang's own <stdbool.h> and <stddef.h>, through the
# runtime, which read no switch of the C library; <stdint.h> and <math.h> come
# after the groups (defect 568). A header added to either is named here, and
# its names are owed to the list and the two runtime files.
test "the headers read before the groups are clang's own two, and the library's come after"
    runtime = read_file("runtime/heroes_runtime.h").must()
    assert c_words.includes_of(runtime) == ["<stdbool.h>", "<stddef.h>", "<stdint.h>"]

    # `<stdint.h>` only for a unit that does not say it reads it after its
    # groups, a unit written before panel 205's R3; every unit this compiler
    # writes says so first.
    assert strings.holds(
        text: runtime,
        needle: "#if !defined(HERO_STANDARD_AFTER_GROUPS)\n#include <stdint.h>\n#endif\n"
    )
    first = read_file("runtime/heroes_unit.h").must()
    assert strings.holds(text: first, needle: "#define HERO_STANDARD_AFTER_GROUPS 1\n")
    assert c_words.includes_of(first) == ["\"heroes_runtime.h\""]
    assert c_words.includes_of(read_file("runtime/hero_os.h").must()) == ["\"heroes_runtime.h\""]
    assert emit_externs.SEEDS == ["heroes_unit.h", "hero_os.h"]
    assert c_words.includes_of(read_file("runtime/heroes_standard.h").must()) == ["\"heroes_reserved_open.h\"", "<stdint.h>", "<math.h>", "\"heroes_reserved_close.h\""]

# The ten macros the unit writes from the library's standard headers (four
# when defect 568 was repaired, defect 567's division guard six more) are
# the ten the guard keeps and `heroes_standard.h` defines again; any other
# macro of `<stdint.h>` or `<math.h>` an emitter module writes is named here,
# owed its guard and its definition after the groups.
test "the unit's own C writes ten macros of the library's standard headers, each guarded and defined again"
    listed: {str: bool} @= {}

    for name in guarded_names.names()
        listed[name] @ true

    standard = read_file("runtime/heroes_standard.h").must()

    for name in STANDARD_USED
        assert !listed[name].is_err()
        assert strings.holds(text: standard, needle: "#ifndef " + name + "\n")

    for name in guarded_names.left()
        assert listed[name].is_err()

    written: [str] @= []
    files = process.files_under("selfhost/emit").must().push("selfhost/emit.hero")

    for path in files
        if strings.ends_with(text: path, suffix: ".hero") && !strings.ends_with(text: path, suffix: "guarded_names.hero")
            quoted = c_words.quoted_words(read_file(path).must())

            for name in guarded_names.left()
                if !quoted[name].is_err()
                    written @ written.push(path + ": " + name)

    assert written.join(" ") == ""

# What a group writes is the header's: the function, the constant, the
# record and its fields, and a releaser a mark names.
test "the names a program's groups write are left to their header"
    text = "extern \"x.h\"\n    function twice(x: i64) -> i64\n    function make() -> cstr owned x_free\n    constant LIMIT: i64\n    record Pair\n        left: i64\n        right: i64\n\nfunction main()\n    print(twice(x: LIMIT))\n"
    s = source.new_source(name: "t.hero", text: text)
    parsed = parse.parse_source(s)
    got = bound_names(s, parsed.decls)

    for name in ["twice", "make", "x_free", "LIMIT", "Pair", "left", "right"]
        assert !got[name].is_err()

    for name in ["main", "print", "x", "i64", "cstr", "function"]
        assert got[name].is_err()

    # The library's bindings of the runtime's own headers are no group's.
    library = source.with_library(
        name: "t.hero",
        user: "function main()\n    print(1)\n",
        library: "extern \"hero_os.h\"\n    constant HERO_OS_OK: i64\n"
    )
    assert bound_names(library, parse.parse_source(library).decls)["HERO_OS_OK"].is_err()

# **The names C reserves that the compiler's own C writes are saved and given
# back by the reserved pair, in the list's order** (defect 2333), around the
# groups inside the guard's two files, around the library's headers
# `heroes_standard.h` reads, and named by `runtime.c`, so the runtime's key
# holds both.
test "the reserved pair saves and gives back the reserved list, inside the guard and around the library's headers (defect 2333)"
    listed = guarded_names.reserved()
    assert c_words.pragmas(text: read_file("runtime/heroes_reserved_open.h").must(), word: "push_macro") == listed
    assert c_words.pragmas(text: read_file("runtime/heroes_reserved_close.h").must(), word: "pop_macro") == listed
    open = c_words.includes_of(read_file("runtime/heroes_guard_open.h").must())
    close = c_words.includes_of(read_file("runtime/heroes_guard_close.h").must())
    assert open == ["\"heroes_reserved_open.h\""]
    assert close == ["\"heroes_reserved_close.h\""]
    whole = c_words.includes_of(read_file("runtime/runtime.c").must())
    named: {str: bool} @= {}

    for one in whole
        named[one] @ true

    assert !named["\"heroes_reserved_open.h\""].is_err()
    assert !named["\"heroes_reserved_close.h\""].is_err()

# **Every name C reserves for the implementation that the C this compiler
# writes holds is in the reserved list** (defect 2333): each such word inside
# an emitter module's string literals, the three overflow builtins
# `emit/operator.hero` composes from `__builtin_`, and each the runtime's
# four headers write. A word the emitter only reads of clang's own answers is
# named here with its reason, and nowhere else: the qualifiers a header's
# type may carry after a pointer (`emit/lend_type.hero` reads them off), and
# the import prefix of a Windows linker's complaint (`emit/ffi_build.hero`),
# and the parameter list of a variadic macro, which `emit/c_words.hero` names
# to leave out of what it reads.
test "every reserved name the emitter writes and the runtime's headers write is in the reserved list (defect 2333)"
    listed: {str: bool} @= {}

    for name in guarded_names.reserved()
        listed[name] @ true

    read_only = [
        "_Nonnull"
        "_Null_unspecified"
        "_Nullable"
        "_Nullable_result"
        "__imp_"
        "__restrict"
        "__restrict__"
        "__VA_ARGS__"
    ]
    exempt: {str: bool} @= {}

    for name in read_only
        exempt[name] @ true

    missing: [str] @= []
    files = process.files_under("selfhost/emit").must().push("selfhost/emit.hero")

    for path in files
        if strings.ends_with(text: path, suffix: ".hero")
            for word in sort(keys(c_words.quoted_words(c_words.without_tests(read_file(path).must()))))
                composed = word == "__builtin_"

                # The prefix `operator.hero` composes the three overflow
                # builtins from, and from nowhere else.
                if composed && !strings.ends_with(text: path, suffix: "/operator.hero")
                    missing @ missing.push(path + ": " + word)
                else if !composed && c_words.is_reserved(word) && listed[word].is_err() && exempt[word].is_err()
                    missing @ missing.push(path + ": " + word)

    for name in ["__builtin_add_overflow", "__builtin_sub_overflow", "__builtin_mul_overflow"]
        if listed[name].is_err()
            missing @ missing.push("operator.hero: " + name)

    for path in ["runtime/heroes_runtime.h", "runtime/hero_os.h", "runtime/heroes_unit.h", "runtime/heroes_standard.h"]
        for word in sort(keys(c_words.reserved_used(read_file(path).must())))
            if listed[word].is_err()
                missing @ missing.push(path + ": " + word)

    assert missing.join(" ") == ""

# **A header's macro over a reserved name the unit writes is given back after
# the groups** (defect 2333), asked of clang: a group's header defines
# `__builtin_add_overflow` and `_Static_assert` to nothing the compiler
# means, and after the guard's close the unit's own C reads both as C's own;
# the same header after the close, where nothing gives them back, is refused,
# so the test is not blind.
test "a group header's macro over a builtin or a keyword the unit writes is given back after the guard (defect 2333)"
    dir = "build/selftest-reserved-guard"
    assert !process.remove_tree(dir).is_err()
    assert !process.mkdir_all(dir).is_err()
    planted = "#define __builtin_add_overflow(a, b, r) hero_planted_no_such_name\n#define _Static_assert(c, m) extern int hero_planted_no_such_name\n"
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/planted.h", text: planted).is_err()
    body = "static int hero_sum(int a) { int r; return __builtin_add_overflow(a, 1, &r) ? -1 : r; }\n_Static_assert(sizeof(int) >= 2, \"the keyword is C's\");\nint hero_reserved_probe(void) { return hero_sum(1); }\n"
    no_names: {str: bool} = {}
    guarded = strings.with_all(list: [FIRST_LINE], more: around(["#include <planted.h>"], bound: no_names)).push(STANDARD_LINE).join("\n") + "\n" + body
    bare = [FIRST_LINE, STANDARD_LINE, "#include <planted.h>"].join("\n") + "\n" + body

    for pair in [[guarded, "true"], [bare, "false"]]
        unit = dir + "/unit-" + pair[1] + ".c"
        assert !write_file(path: unit, text: pair[0]).is_err()
        said = process.run(
            program: "clang",
            words: ["clang", "-std=gnu11", "-Werror", "-fsyntax-only", "-I", "runtime", "-I", dir, unit],
            out: process.discard(),
            err: process.discard()
        )
        assert said.started
        assert said.code == 0 == (pair[1] == "true")

    assert !process.remove_tree(dir).is_err()

# **Every probe that writes a reserved name after a group's headers reads them
# between the reserved pair** (defect 2333): in each writer named here, every
# `c_text.includes` of its headers stands inside `reserved_around`, so a
# header's macro over `_Static_assert` or a builtin leaves its questions as the
# compiler wrote them. A writer gaining a bare one is named, and so is one
# whose count moved, so the list is looked at again.
test "every probe that writes a reserved name after its headers reads them between the reserved pair (defect 2333)"
    sites = [
        ["selfhost/emit/ffi_asked.hero", "1"]
        ["selfhost/emit/layout_screen.hero", "2"]
        ["selfhost/emit/layout_check.hero", "1"]
        ["selfhost/emit/lock_screen.hero", "1"]
        ["selfhost/emit/layout_text.hero", "1"]
        ["selfhost/cli/header_types.hero", "2"]
        ["selfhost/cli/two_ways_ask.hero", "1"]
        ["selfhost/cli/switch_ask.hero", "1"]
        ["selfhost/cli/unswitch_ask.hero", "1"]
    ]
    wrong: [str] @= []

    for site in sites
        code = c_words.without_tests(read_file(site[0]).must())
        wrapped = c_words.occurrences(text: code, word: "reserved_around(c_text.includes(")
        every = c_words.occurrences(text: code, word: "c_text.includes(")

        if wrapped.to_str() != site[1] || every != wrapped
            wrong @ wrong.push(site[0] + " " + wrapped.to_str() + " of " + every.to_str())

    assert wrong.join(" ") == ""
